---
description : Handles large GPT processing via /supabase_generic_gpt_calls/ when Edge Functions are limited by context size, timeout, or quotas
alwaysApply: false
---

When certain tasks require GPT calls, they can be handled through an Edge Function. For small amounts of data, the simplest approach is to call OpenAI directly. However, when processing larger content, several limitations arise:
	•	The context window may be too small
	•	Even when chunked, Edge Functions have relatively short timeouts
	•	There can be quota limitations with our GPT plans (especially reagrding tokens / minutes)

To address these issues, it’s best to use a dedicated backend endpoint: /supabase_generic_gpt_calls/ that will properly queued these tasks.

To use this endpoint (explained in detail below), ensure that:
	•	Your chunked data is stored in an ARRAY of TEXT within a column.
	•	A row has aldone been inserted with a unique id (UUID), which will be passed to the function.
	•	You have a status column (or similar) that can be updated to reflect progress.
	•	You include an empty column to store the final result, which can be of type TEXT, ARRAY of TEXT, or ARRAY of JSONB, depending on the use case.
    • Using a array as output can be also quite interresting to paginate results on frontend side. To do so, you should call the RPC 

    const { data, error } = await supabase.rpc('get_row_with_sliced_arrays', {
        p_table: 'public.my_table',
        p_id: '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000', // ton UUID
        p_columns_to_slice: ['input', 'output'],       // colonnes à paginer
        p_offset: 0,
        p_limit: 10
    });

if (error) {
  console.error(error);
} else {
  console.log('row:', data.row);
  console.log('meta:', data.meta); //useful to fetch next offset e.g
}



